# Citation metadata. GitHub renders a "Cite this repository" box from this file,
# and Zenodo reads it when minting the DOI.
#
# BEFORE RELEASE, set three things (VERSIONING.md explains how releases are cited):
#   1. date-released  -> the actual date you tag the release
#   2. repository-code / url -> your real GitHub URL
#   3. orcid -> your ORCID iD if you register one (recommended; free, ~5 minutes,
#      at https://orcid.org/register). It is a permanent author identifier that
#      ties this kit, the article, and anything later to one verifiable identity.
#      If you do not register one, delete the orcid line entirely.

cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Mistake-Proofed Quality Records: starter kit"
message: >-
  If you use this kit, please cite it using the metadata in this file.
type: dataset  # CFF 1.2.0 offers only "software" or "dataset"; this kit is
               # documentation, and is deposited to Zenodo as a technical note.
authors:
  - family-names: Mushangwe
    given-names: Tererai
    orcid: "{{ORCID}}"
abstract: >-
  A free, MIT-licensed low-code pattern for building enforceable quality records
  in small and mid-sized manufacturing. A hard gate is a rule the software
  enforces rather than a policy people are asked to remember: a record cannot
  advance to the next stage until the evidence that stage requires actually
  exists. The kit documents a three-table data model, the gate-enforcement
  pattern and the three mistakes that make a gate decorative, a step-by-step
  implementation guide, an explicit mapping to ISO 9001:2015 clauses and
  CMMC / NIST SP 800-171 practices (including what it does not cover).
  Four complete worked examples are included, with fabricated sample data
  throughout — incoming inspection,
  non-conformance intake, in-process production checks, and a read-only
  compliance-status dashboard — together with a complete build guide for
  Microsoft Lists including each gate written as a server-evaluated list
  validation formula, a rollout runbook, a validation and test plan, a
  thirty-minute gate self-test usable against systems not built from this kit,
  and workshop and talk material for teaching it.
  Written for quality managers, engineers and owners at small manufacturers in
  regulated supply chains who have been told they need better records and have
  no budget for a quality-management platform.
keywords:
  - quality management
  - ISO 9001
  - CMMC
  - NIST SP 800-171
  - low-code
  - citizen development
  - manufacturing
  - small and medium-sized manufacturers
  - compliance
  - traceability
  - supply chain
  - process control
  - audit evidence
license: MIT
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "{{RELEASE_DATE}}"
repository-code: "https://github.com/terimush/mistake-proofed-records-starter-kit"
url: "https://github.com/terimush/mistake-proofed-records-starter-kit"
